{"name":"Rum Custard","hex":"#e9cfaa","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"rum","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-2/B language code for Romanian."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A spirit distilled from various preparations of sugarcane, particularly fermented cane sugar and molasses.","A serving of rum."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/rum"},{"term":"custard","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A type of sauce made from milk and eggs (and usually sugar, and sometimes vanilla or other flavourings) and thickened by heat, served hot poured over desserts, as a filling for some pies and cakes, or cold and solidified; also used as a base for some savoury dishes, such as quiches, or eaten as a stand-alone dessert; egg custard.","A similar sauce made from milk and cornflour or custard powder (and usually sugar, and sometimes vanilla or other flavourings) and thickened by heat."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/custard"}],"entries":[{"title":"Rum","blocks":[{"text":"Rum is a liquor made by fermenting and then distilling sugarcane molasses or sugarcane juice. The distillate, initially a clear liquid, is often aged in barrels. Rum originated in the Caribbean in the 17th century, where it was likely first created by enslaved people on sugar plantations, but has come to be produced in nearly every major sugar-producing region of the world."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/A_glass_of_rum.jpg/330px-A_glass_of_rum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rum","wikidata":"Q83376"},{"title":"Custard","blocks":[{"text":"Custard is a variety of culinary preparations based on sweetened milk, cheese, or cream cooked with egg or egg yolk to thicken it, and sometimes also flour, corn starch, or gelatin. Depending on the recipe, custard may vary in consistency from a thin pouring sauce to the thick pastry cream used to fill éclairs. The most common custards are used in custard desserts or dessert sauces and typically include sugar and vanilla; however, savory custards are also found, e.g., in quiche."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Custard.jpg/330px-Custard.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Custard","wikidata":"Q625825"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Rum_display_in_liquor_store.jpg/500px-Rum_display_in_liquor_store.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Rum_display_in_liquor_store.jpg/1280px-Rum_display_in_liquor_store.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Rum_display_in_liquor_store.jpg/500px-Rum_display_in_liquor_store.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Rum display in a liquor store (United States, 2009)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARum_display_in_liquor_store.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Government_house_rum.jpg/500px-Government_house_rum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Government_house_rum.jpg/1280px-Government_house_rum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Government_house_rum.jpg/500px-Government_house_rum.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Government House rum, manufactured by the Virgin Islands Company distillery in Saint Croix, circa 1941","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGovernment_house_rum.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikim